[candidate · 5:31] Mostly, but there's a gap. I framed costs as COGS, labor, occupancy, and overhead — that's clean and non-overlapping on the operating side. But the metric we're solving for is net profit margin, not operating margin. So I'm missing everything below the operating line: interest expense, taxes, depreciation and amortization, and any one-time items like impairments or restructuring charges. If the company took on debt or wrote something down, net margin could crater while operations look fine. I should add a "non-operating" branch to make it exhaustive.

One more thing I'd flag: revenue is MECE as price times volume, but volume itself splits into transactions per store and items per transaction — and with the footprint flat at 200 stores, that distinction matters a lot. All of the 15% growth is same-store, so either we're selling more or charging more, and those have very different cost implications.
